== 11. Christ Delights in His People’s Secret Prayers

Eleventhly, consider that Christ is greatly moved by and delighted in the secret prayers of His people. “O my dove, in the clefts of the rock, in the secret places of the stairs, let me see your face, let me hear your voice; for your voice is sweet, and your countenance is lovely” (Song 2:14). Christ watches His spouse when she is in the clefts of the rock. When she has gone off into a corner to pray, He looks on her with singular delight and with special intimations of His love. Nothing is more sweet, delightful, and welcome to Christ than the secret services of His people. Their secret breathings are like lovely songs to Him (Mal 3:4); their secret prayers in the clefts of the rock, or under the stairs, are like sweet incense to Jesus. The spouse withdraws to the secret places of the stairs not only for safety, but also for secrecy, so that she might pour out her soul into the bosom of her beloved more freely, without suspicion of hypocrisy. The great delight that parents take in the secret lispings and whisperings of their children is no delight compared with the delight Christ takes in the secret prayers of His people. And therefore, if you#super[+] want to be friends and furtherers of Christ’s delight, be much in secret prayer.
